{"answer":"The proof is invalid as a proof that √2 is rational. The inference that both a and b are even is valid only after supplying the prime-divisibility lemma: if 2 divides n², then 2 divides n. But both being even contradicts the assumption that a/b is in lowest terms. Thus the assumed lowest-terms representation cannot exist; the argument is a contradiction proof of the irrationality of √2, not a proof of rationality.","approaches":[{"description":"Assume a lowest-terms integer representation √2=a/b, use prime divisibility to show 2 divides a and then 2 divides b, and compare this with the lowest-terms condition.","id":"contradiction-proof","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It supplies the missing divisibility justification and yields a contradiction, which is the appropriate conclusion."},{"description":"Treat the conclusion that a and b are both even as evidence that √2=a/b is a valid rational representation.","id":"rationality-claim","outcome":"rejected","reason":"A common factor 2 violates the stated lowest-terms condition, so this conclusion refutes the assumed representation rather than confirms it."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Temporary assumption for reductio.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["step-discharge"],"id":"lowest-representation","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["step-suppose","step-square","step-a-even","step-substitute","step-b-even","step-conflict"],"statement":"Temporarily suppose that there are integers a and b, with b nonzero, such that √2=a/b and a/b is in lowest terms.","step_ids":["step-suppose"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"additional_assumption","basis_reference":"Use the usual meaning of “in lowest terms”: no integer d>1 divides both a and b.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"lowest-terms-meaning","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For this assessment, “a/b is in lowest terms” means that a and b have no common positive divisor greater than 1.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by squaring the temporary equality.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["lowest-representation"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"squared-equation","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The temporary representation implies a²=2b².","step_ids":["step-square"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"standard_result","basis_reference":"Prime-square divisibility lemma: for a prime p and integer n, p divides n² only if p divides n; applied with p=2 in step-a-even and step-b-even.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"prime-square-lemma","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The required divisibility justification is the prime-square divisibility lemma, applied to 2 dividing a² and later to 2 dividing b².","step_ids":["step-a-even","step-b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from a²=2b² using the prime-square divisibility lemma.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["squared-equation","prime-square-lemma"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"a-is-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The temporary representation implies that a is even.","step_ids":["step-a-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived after substituting a=2c and applying the prime-square divisibility lemma.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["a-is-even","prime-square-lemma"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"b-is-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The temporary representation implies that b is even.","step_ids":["step-substitute","step-b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Both a and b are divisible by 2, contrary to the stipulated lowest-terms meaning.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["lowest-terms-meaning","a-is-even","b-is-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"lowest-terms-contradiction","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The temporary lowest-terms representation produces a contradiction.","step_ids":["step-conflict"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Reductio discharges the temporary representation assumption.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["lowest-terms-contradiction"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"no-lowest-representation","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"There is no lowest-terms integer fraction a/b with b nonzero satisfying a²=2b², and hence no such fraction equal to √2.","step_ids":["step-discharge"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The claimed rationality conclusion is incompatible with the discharged contradiction proof.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["no-lowest-representation"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"validity-assessment","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The stated argument does not establish that √2 is rational; with the missing lemma supplied, it establishes the opposite conclusion under the usual lowest-terms interpretation.","step_ids":["step-discharge"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-suppose","justification":"This is the proposed rational representation, taken temporarily in order to test its consistency with the lowest-terms condition.","statement":"Suppose integers a,b with b nonzero satisfy √2=a/b and that a/b is in lowest terms."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-suppose"],"id":"step-square","justification":"Squaring √2=a/b gives 2=a²/b²; multiplying by b² gives a²=2b².","statement":"Derive a²=2b²."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-square"],"id":"step-a-even","justification":"The equation shows 2 divides a². Apply the prime-square divisibility lemma with p=2 to obtain 2 divides a.","statement":"Conclude that a is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-a-even","step-square"],"id":"step-substitute","justification":"Since a is even, write a=2c for an integer c. Substitution gives 4c²=2b², hence b²=2c².","statement":"Rewrite the equation as b²=2c² for some integer c."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-substitute"],"id":"step-b-even","justification":"The rewritten equation shows 2 divides b². Apply the same prime-square divisibility lemma with p=2.","statement":"Conclude that b is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-a-even","step-b-even"],"id":"step-conflict","justification":"If a and b are even, then 2 is a common positive divisor of a and b, which is forbidden by the stated meaning of lowest terms.","statement":"Obtain a contradiction with the lowest-terms condition."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-conflict"],"id":"step-discharge","justification":"The contradiction was derived from the temporary proposed lowest-terms representation, so that representation cannot exist.","statement":"Discharge the temporary assumption and reject the alleged rational representation."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
